    { q: "How do I wire two 12V batteries for 24V?", a: "Connect the positive terminal of one battery to the negative terminal of the other; the remaining terminals provide 24V across the pair." },
    { q: "Do 24V batteries need a special charger?", a: "Yes. Use a charger rated for 24V, or charge each 12V battery separately with a 12V charger." },
    { q: "What CCA do 24V truck systems need?", a: "Heavy 24V trucks typically need 850–1100+ CCA per battery, depending on engine size and climate. Confirm the exact figure in your vehicle manual." },
    { q: "What happens if I mix an old and a new battery in a 24V pair?", a: "The pair charges unevenly: the stronger battery is overcharged and the weaker one undercharged, shortening the life of both. Always use two identical batteries of the same model, age and rating." },
    { q: "Can I jump-start a 24V truck from a 12V vehicle?", a: "Not directly across the full 24V system. Jump-start procedures for 24V systems vary by vehicle; follow the OEM procedure, and if unsure, charge each 12V battery individually or call a professional." },
    { q: "Do 24V systems use a single 24V battery?", a: "Most heavy trucks use two 12V batteries in series rather than a single 24V unit, because 12V batteries are more widely available and each can be serviced or replaced individually. Dedicated 24V monobloc units exist but are less common in trucks." }
